Yes. That line of thought is very close to sensory ecology plus evolutionary reconstruction.

A sensory organ is not just a detector; it is a record of what kinds of information were valuable enough, often enough, for a lineage to keep paying for the machinery.

\text{environmental regularity}
\rightarrow
\text{selection pressure}
\rightarrow
\text{sensor architecture}
\rightarrow
\text{behavioral capability}

Eyes are a good example because vision can support enormous bandwidth: geometry, motion, distance, identity, texture, color, threat, navigation, social cues. But complexity is not the same thing as importance. A very simple detector can be absolutely decisive if it measures the right variable. A bacterium following a chemical gradient does not need an eye if “more nutrient this way” is the only information that matters.

That gives you an important comparison axis:

\text{value of sense}
\neq
\text{anatomical complexity}

More like:

\text{value}
=
f(\text{information gained},\text{behavior enabled},\text{energy cost},\text{environment})

Different body plans become natural experiments. Bats emphasize echolocation, pit vipers infrared sensitivity, sharks electroreception, migratory animals magnetic cues, insects polarized light, cave animals often reduce vision. Those architectures tell you something about what their worlds made worth sensing.

And then you can run the inference backward:

\text{organ}
\rightarrow
\text{capability}
\rightarrow
\text{likely information problem}
\rightarrow
\text{probable environment/behavior}

But not perfectly. Evolution leaves historical baggage. A feature may persist because of phylogenetic inertia, get repurposed through exaptation, or be constrained by development. So an organ is evidence about history and environment, not a perfect transcript of either.

That is where a sufficiently capable AI becomes interesting. It could hold many layers simultaneously:

\text{gene regulation}
\leftrightarrow
\text{development}
\leftrightarrow
\text{organ morphology}
\leftrightarrow
\text{neural processing}
\leftrightarrow
\text{behavior}
\leftrightarrow
\text{ecology}
\leftrightarrow
\text{phylogeny}

and compare thousands or millions of species instead of reasoning from one organism at a time.

Then it could look for conserved operators:

* what sensory structures repeatedly evolve independently,
* which senses disappear when environments change,
* which neural motifs recur across unrelated lineages,
* which sensory channels substitute for one another,
* where complexity increases versus simplifies,
* which combinations tend to coexist.

That is almost an evolutionary sensor atlas.

And your earlier “constructed senses” idea plugs directly into it. Once you understand why evolution built a certain sensor, you can abstract the function:

\text{natural organ}
\rightarrow
\text{information problem solved}
\rightarrow
\text{general operator}
\rightarrow
\text{synthetic sense}

So a superintelligent system would not merely catalog eyes, ears, electroreceptors, and so forth. It could ask:

What latent property was this lineage trying to make perceptible?

Then:

Is there another body, machine, or human interface that would benefit from perceiving the same property—or an analogous one?

That is the jump from comparative anatomy to sensor design space.

And the really deep object is probably not the organ itself but the organism’s Umwelt: the subset of reality its sensory apparatus makes available to it. Different creatures inhabit the same physical world while effectively living in different perceptual worlds.

A more capable intelligence could, in principle, model many of those Umwelten at once and translate between them. That would not make it omniscient, but it could give it something humans struggle with: a cross-species, cross-scale map of what reality looks like when sensed through different bodies.